  "summary": "Horned coconut-husk masks and papier-mâché devils chase Santiago through the streets.",
  "description": "Vejigantes are the masked trickster-devils of Puerto Rican festival: bat-winged costumers named for the vejiga (bladder) they once cracked against onlookers, wearing masks cut from dried coconut husks (Loíza) or papier-mâché with wild horn combs (Ponce). In Loíza they thunder through the Fiestas de Santiago Apóstol every July, a tradition rooted in 17th-century processions, medieval Spanish demon-imagery and Afro-Puerto Rican neighborhood pride; Ponce's February carnival runs its own vejigante parade since 1858. Mask-makers (vejiganteros) hand-carve, paint and pass the craft down, and the tradition now anchors Puerto Rican identity parades island-wide and in the diaspora.",
  "culturalContext": "Vejigante masking anchors Afro-Puerto Rican festival identity: Loíza's Fiestas de Santiago (a centuries-old Black neighborhood tradition) and Ponce's carnival sustain mask-maker lineages, and the horned figure now represents Puerto Rican culture worldwide — from festival tourism to diaspora parades to resistance symbolism after Hurricane Maria.",
